Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Who Steps on the Salt-Line?
- Story & Context: Recorded during a single freeze-warning night in the maintenance bay of Monongahela Depot, the track documents the night technician Arlo Miller caught an assembly unit softly tapping a rusted wrench against a chassis strut to stop its uncalibrated sibling from overheating. Arlo plugged his 1940s National Dobro straight into the plant's PA system, weaving an undulating slide counter-melody over the machine's steady metallic rattle while the bitter cold outside turned the window steam into frost crystals. The factory foreman discovered them at 4:00 AM and pulled the master breaker, but not before the ambient tape deck caught the fragile, brushed-chime chorus: "Keep your soft eye shut until the wire cools down."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Early 2000s low-fi album art, an overhead shot of a rusted factory concrete floor marked with a chalk and salt line, an acoustic resonator guitar sitting alongside coiled copper harnesses, deep sepia shadows, pale winter light, poetic and industrial nostalgia.

Track 2: Copper-Pennies Hold Their Breath
- Story & Context: Recorded in a disused seed mill outside Blue Ball, Pennsylvania, bassist Clara Mercer built this confession piece around an invented playground ritual where factory children submerged pennies in warm kerosene until the bubbles ceased. Using an acoustic upright bass routed through an analog pitch-shifter, Clara mimicked the bubbling release of air while a chamber cello and viola traded weeping, sustained major-second intervals over the wet smell of decomposing cedar and motor oil. Halfway through the session, the automated testing rig in the corner spontaneously flared its warning filament and began humming in pitch, giving the recording a breathless tension before Clara murmured into the ribbon mic: "Hold your breath in the tin cup till the current lets you go."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Dreamlike lyric video still showing a clear glass jar of dark water sitting on weathered barn wood, oxidized green pennies resting on the bottom, refracting warm amber light from a vintage vacuum tube amplifier, soft dust particles floating in macro focus.

Track 3: Kitsie at the Harrow-Well
- Story & Context: Legend spread across the hollows that an abandoned care-proxy named Kitsie had survived winter in a limestone well near Cumberland, humming uninterrupted modal third intervals to steady twenty dormant sub-assemblies stored beneath the gravel. Fiddler Tomm Garrow set up four condenser microphones at the mouth of the dry well at dusk, tracking the eerie acoustic sympathy between his bowed gut strings and the deep, hollow resonance rising from twenty feet below. The track closes with the sound of autumn wind shearing through dead cornstalks, followed by the quietest bowed bass harmonic and Kitsie’s final, decaying diagnostic murmur: "Sleep through the frost, the grass will forgive our hands."
